# DURABLE REPRO — indexing a `Set[T]` returns a RAW ADDRESS. No `&` anywhere;
# this is not a sigil bug.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, C backend): `gg check` reports "OK: no semantic
# errors", the program builds, and `print(s[0])` emits something like
# `187651201204896` — a pointer, printed as an int. The digits are
# ASLR-dependent; the stable fact is that it is an address, not the element.
#
# Found while censusing the CollectionKind axis for the `&`-of-projection work:
# `try_resolve_index_element_ptr` admits only `Array` and `OrderedMap`, so `Set`
# / `OrderedSet` / `Map` fall outside every place-resolution path. That is the
# *lowering* half; this fixture pins the more basic question of what `s[0]` on a
# Set is supposed to MEAN at all.
#
# INTENDED: a check-time REJECTION. A `Set` has no positional index — there is
# no defensible element to return for `s[0]`, so the reference-grade answer is
# `gg check` refusing the program, not a silently-emitted address. (If indexing a
# Set is ever given a meaning, this fixture becomes the pin for that meaning.)
# The fixture therefore asserts the REJECT, not a value.
#
# ⚠ Out of scope for the `&`-of-projection track — filed separately so it does
# not ride on a fix that cannot reach it.

int seed():
    return 10

void main():
    Set[int] s = Set[int]()
    s.add(seed())
    print(s[0])
